0. Reference availability
==============================================================================
  PyRhO importable in THIS interpreter: False
  detail: TypeError: descriptor '__setitem__' requires a 'collections.OrderedDict' object but received a 'PyRhOparameters'

  Stored PyRhO reference found: version 0.9.4, numpy 1.19.5, 20001 samples
  Produced in a separate Python 3.9 environment because PyRhO cannot
  run on 3.12. PyRhO itself was not modified.

==============================================================================
1. Numerics: fixed-step RK4 against the exact solution
==============================================================================
  10.0 mW/mm^2 = 2.3660e+16 photons/mm^2/s
  Ga 0.400626  Gd 0.104000  Gr 0.032006  (per ms)

  Under constant light the rates are constant, so the system is linear
  and expm(At)x0 is exact. This is a test of the integration, not of the
  biology.

  samples compared:                20001
  max |O_numeric - O_exact|:       1.054e-12  (at t = 2.55 ms)
  as a fraction of the peak:       1.680e-12
  max |C+O+D - 1|:                 7.994e-15

  VERDICT: PASS against a 1e-06 absolute tolerance on the open fraction.
  The integrator reproduces the exact solution to well under the
  precision any parameter here is known to, so integration error is
  not a limit on anything this package reports.

==============================================================================
2. Algebra: converged open fraction against PyRhO's closed form
==============================================================================
  PyRhO documents I_SS proportional to Ga*Gr / (Gd*(Gr+Ga) + Ga*Gr).
  Read from its source, not executed. This checks the algebra against the
  reference's own stated result; it cannot check the transient.

  closed form:                     0.2217794146
  converged exact solution:        0.2217794146
  absolute deviation:              4.285e-13
  relative deviation:              1.932e-12

  VERDICT: PASS

==============================================================================
3. Agreement: this implementation against PyRhO, trace for trace
==============================================================================
  reference: PyRhO 0.9.4 (Python 3.9 env)
  samples compared:                20001
  peak O, this package:            0.627224622
  peak O, PyRhO:                   0.627224638
  max |O_optosim - O_pyrho|:       3.389e-08  (at t = 1.76 ms)
  as a fraction of the peak:       5.403e-08

  VERDICT: PASS against a 0.001 absolute tolerance on the open fraction.
  The independently written three-state equations reproduce PyRhO's
  transient. The residual is at the scale of odeint's own default
  tolerance (~1.5e-8), so it is the reference's integration error
  rather than a disagreement in the equations -- this bounds
  agreement at that level rather than proving exact equality.

  What it rules out, which the steady state could not: a
  transcription error shifting Ga and Gr in compensating
  directions. Those have different Hill exponents (p 0.8, q 0.25)
  and scale differently with flux, so a cancellation at one
  irradiance would not survive a tenfold change -- run this at both
  1 and 10 mW/mm^2 and both agree.

==============================================================================
4. What this does and does not establish
==============================================================================
  Established: the photocycle is integrated correctly (exact solution),
  the steady state agrees with PyRhO's published closed form, and -- when
  a reference is present -- the full transient agrees with PyRhO's own
  integration of its own equations.

  NOT established: that the model describes real ChR2. Agreeing with
  PyRhO means the equations were transcribed correctly, not that the
  three-state photocycle is right about the protein. The peak-to-plateau
  ratio (2.22 at 1 mW/mm^2, 2.83 at 10) sits in the range reported for
  ChR2, which is encouraging and is still not a comparison against a
  recording. Validating against measured photocurrents is a separate job.

  This is the shape of chemdisco's redocking result: the mechanics check
  out, and the harder question is labelled rather than answered by a
  number nobody measured.

Irradiance is specified AT THE OPSIN. Propagation through tissue is not modelled: no absorption, scattering or depth attenuation. Blue light falls off steeply in brain tissue, so these values are not the output of a light source at the surface and must not be read as one. Tissue optics is a future layer, not an effect already included.

